Title: Delay-load dbghelp and shell32 so XP's loader accepts node.exe
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

  The procedure entry point SymGetSearchPathW could not be located in the
  dynamic link library DBGHELP.dll

On XP, after the manifest in patch 14 got the image as far as a console
window. Nothing calls SymGetSearchPathW during startup; the loader simply
binds every static import before the entry point runs, and XP's dbghelp.dll
5.1 predates the Unicode symbol-path functions.

The structural difference against the Electron build, which has run on XP all
along, is not which functions it uses but how it imports them:

  electron.exe  static:  9 DLLs    delay: 48 DLLs (dbghelp, iphlpapi, shell32,
                                          ole32, user32, userenv, advapi32 ...)
  node.exe      static: 11 DLLs    delay:  0

Chromium delay-loads nearly all of it, so a pre-Vista export only matters if
something actually calls it. node links everything statically, so every
pre-Vista export is fatal at load whether reachable or not.

All 13 of node's dbghelp imports are diagnostic-only, from three places, none
of them on a startup path:

  - src/debug_utils.cc, via NativeSymbolDebuggingContext::New(), which runs
    when a backtrace is printed
  - deps/v8/src/base/debug/stack_trace_win.cc, whose InitializeSymbols() is
    guarded by g_initialized_symbols and called from StackTrace::Print. Node
    never calls EnableInProcessStackDumping or constructs a base::debug
    StackTrace at all
  - deps/uv/src/win/process.c, where uv__kill writes a minidump for a killed
    child, behind a LocalDumps registry lookup. That is also the only caller
    of SHGetKnownFolderPath, node's single shell32 import, which is why
    shell32 is delay-loaded here too

So deferring both costs nothing anywhere and matches what Chromium does for
dbghelp exactly -- its config("delayloads") is config("delayloads_except_
dbghelp") plus /DELAYLOAD:dbghelp.dll.

iphlpapi is deliberately left static even though its 10 pre-Vista imports are
just as fatal. c-ares calls GetBestRoute2, NotifyIpInterfaceChange and
GetUnicastIpAddressTable while bringing a channel up, so delay-loading would
turn a refusal to start into a crash on the first name resolution. c-ares
gates all three on HAVE_GETBESTROUTE2, HAVE_NOTIFYIPINTERFACECHANGE and
HAVE_CONVERTINTERFACEINDEXTOLUID and carries fallbacks for each, so that group
belongs in a config patch rather than this one.

This does not make node.exe start on XP on its own. 26 kernel32 imports remain
fatal and cannot be delay-loaded, kernel32 being mapped before any delay-load
helper could run; those are winwrap's job.

diff --git a/node.gypi b/node.gypi
index 6587270c..a9a5a4c8 100644
--- a/node.gypi
+++ b/node.gypi
@@ -61,6 +61,64 @@
         }],
       ],
     }],
+    [ 'OS=="win" and "<(_type)"=="executable"', {
+      'msvs_settings': {
+        'VCLinkerTool': {
+          # Both of these are reached only from diagnostic paths, and both have
+          # exports that do not exist before Vista -- so importing them
+          # statically means the loader refuses the image on XP over functions
+          # the process would never have called. dbghelp supplies the symbol
+          # and minidump machinery used by debug_utils.cc, by V8's
+          # stack_trace_win.cc and by libuv's child-process crash dumper, and
+          # of its 13 imports XP's dbghelp.dll 5.1 is missing at least
+          # SymGetSearchPathW and SymSetSearchPathW. shell32 is here for one
+          # function, SHGetKnownFolderPath, which libuv calls in the same
+          # crash dumper to find %LOCALAPPDATA% and which arrived in Vista.
+          #
+          # Nothing calls either at startup. V8's InitializeSymbols() is
+          # guarded by g_initialized_symbols and reached only from
+          # StackTrace::Print; node's Win32SymbolDebuggingContext is
+          # constructed by NativeSymbolDebuggingContext::New(), which runs when
+          # a backtrace is dumped; and libuv's uses sit inside uv__kill behind
+          # a LocalDumps registry lookup. So deferring the binding costs
+          # nothing on any Windows and is what Chromium already does --
+          # config("delayloads") there is config("delayloads_except_dbghelp")
+          # plus /DELAYLOAD:dbghelp.dll, which is why Electron's XP build
+          # imports dbghelp lazily and starts where this did not.
+          #
+          # Not iphlpapi, which stays a static import and needs no help to be
+          # one. It had 10 pre-Vista imports. c-ares accounted for 8 -- it
+          # called GetBestRoute2, NotifyIpInterfaceChange and
+          # GetUnicastIpAddressTable to bring a channel up -- and
+          # CARES_NO_VISTA_IPHLPAPI in deps/cares/cares.gyp removes those by
+          # selecting fallbacks c-ares already carries. The other 2 are
+          # libuv's uv_if_indextoname, wanting ConvertInterfaceIndexToLuid and
+          # ConvertInterfaceLuidToNameW, and winwrap supplies both (its
+          # src/net.cc), so they never reach iphlpapi's import list at all.
+          # What node.exe still imports from iphlpapi, XP exports.
+          #
+          # Delay-loading it would have hidden the 10 rather than dealt with
+          # them: a clean failure at load traded for a crash on the first
+          # name resolution.
+          'DelayLoadDLLs': [
+            'dbghelp.dll',
+            'shell32.dll',
+          ],
+          # __delayLoadHelper2 lives here; /DELAYLOAD does not pull it in by
+          # itself. node-gyp's addon.gypi links it the same way for the same
+          # reason, as does Chromium in build/config/BUILD.gn.
+          'AdditionalDependencies': [
+            'delayimp.lib',
+          ],
+          # LNK4199, "/DELAYLOAD:x ignored; no imports found". node.exe imports
+          # from both, but the smaller executables sharing this file do not all
+          # import from either, and the warning says nothing in that case.
+          'AdditionalOptions': [
+            '/ignore:4199',
+          ],
+        },
+      },
+    }],
     [ 'OS=="win"', {
       'defines!': [
         'NODE_PLATFORM="win"',
